==================================================
Lab 1: SQL Injection in WHERE Clause (Hidden Data)
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website shows products by category. When you click a category, the website takes that category name
and puts it straight into a SQL query to fetch products from the database. The website does not check or clean this input,
so we can change the query by typing special characters.

Goal: make the website show products that are not released yet.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a user clicks the Gifts category, the browser sends a request with category equal to Gifts.

The website then runs this query:

SELECT * FROM products WHERE category = ‘Gifts’ AND released = 1

This means:

get all columns
from the products table
only rows where category is Gifts
and only rows where released equals 1

Products that are not released have released equal to 0, so normally they stay hidden.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The website has no protection against SQL injection. Whatever text we put in place of the category value goes directly into the query.
So we can add our own SQL code through it.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Single quote: In SQL, a single quote starts or ends a piece of text. If we add our own single quote,
we end the text early, and anything after it is read as SQL code, not just text.
Double dash: This is a comment symbol in SQL. Anything written after it on that line is ignored by the database.
OR 1=1: The number 1 always equals 1, so this part is always true. When this is added with OR, the whole condition becomes true for every row in the table.

======================================
ATTACK 1: REMOVE THE RELEASED CHECK
======================================
Instead of sending just Gifts as the category, we send Gifts followed by a single quote and two dashes.

The query becomes:

SELECT * FROM products WHERE category = ‘Gifts’--‘ AND released = 1

The two dashes turn the rest of the line into a comment, so the part checking released equal to 1 is ignored completely.
Now the website shows Gifts products whether they are released or not.

================================================
ATTACK 2: SHOW ALL PRODUCTS FROM ALL CATEGORIES
================================================

Instead of just closing the quote, we send Gifts followed by a single quote, then OR 1 equals 1, then two dashes.

The query becomes:

SELECT * FROM products WHERE category = ‘Gifts’ OR 1=1--‘ AND released = 1

Because 1 always equals 1, this condition is true for every single row in the table, no matter what category it belongs to.
The two dashes again remove the released check. So the website shows every product from every category, released or not.

Note: when this payload is sent through a web address, spaces are usually written as a plus sign, so OR shows up with plus signs
around it instead of normal spaces. This is just how browsers write spaces inside addresses, the actual meaning is still a normal space.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and click on any category.
Look at the address bar, find the part that says category equals something.
Change that value to: Gifts followed by a single quote, then OR 1 equals 1, then two dashes.
Press enter. All products appear, and the lab marks itself as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

It imports the requests library, which lets Python send web requests, the same way a browser does.
* It asks you to type or paste the lab’s address, and saves it as task_url. You are expected to paste the address
already trimmed up to the word filter, as the comment above it says.
* It builds the payload. The payload is the category value that will be sent: Gifts followed by a single quote,
then OR 1 equals 1, then two dashes. This is stored inside a dictionary called params, under the key category.
* It prints a message showing which address the payload is being sent to, just so you can see what is happening while the script runs.
* It sends a request to the address you entered, using requests.get, and attaches the params to it.
The requests library automatically turns the space into a plus sign or percent two zero, and turns the single quote into percent two seven, 
so the address stays valid even though it has special characters in it.
* It reads the response that comes back from the website, which is the actual page content.
* It checks if the page contains the sentence that the lab displays when it has been solved. If that sentence is found,
it prints a success message. If not, it prints a failure message.

=============================
WHY THIS VERSION WORKS WELL
=============================

Asking for the address with input means you do not need to edit the code every time you start a new lab instance,
you just paste the new address when the script asks for it.
Keeping the payload inside params instead of writing it directly into the address keeps the code clean and avoids mistakes with encoding,
since the library handles that part for you.

=============================
 ONE THING TO KEEP IN MIND
=============================

The comment above task_url tells you to trim the address till the word filter. This means when you copy the address from your browser,
you should remove everything after filter, including any question mark or extra text, and paste only the part that ends with filter.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The website builds the query by joining plain text together, something like this in plain words: it takes the fixed starting part of the query,
joins it with whatever the user typed, then joins the rest of the fixed query after it. Because of this joining, whatever the user types becomes
part of the actual SQL instructions, not just a harmless piece of text.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

The fix is to use something called parameterized queries, also called prepared statements. With this method, the place for the user
input is marked separately from the SQL instructions, and the database always treats that input as plain text, never as code, no matter
what characters are inside it.

Other good practices:

Only allow known, expected values for things like category names.
Give the database account used by the website the smallest possible permissions.
Avoid building queries by joining text together manually.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Never trust anything typed by a user.
A single quote ends a text value early, two dashes turn the rest into a comment, and OR 1=1 makes a condition always true.
Hiding data using a simple condition like released equal to 1 does not actually protect it if someone can change the query itself.
